<card id="c" title="Zla Kolata and Prokletije: 300 Euros There and Back">
<do type="prev" label="Back"><prev/></do>
<p><i><b>P.S.</b> Buy your bus ticket at the ticket office, on the station&apos;s official website bas.rs or at the ticket machine: then platform access is included in the price, with no line. And get to the station an hour early.</i></p>
<p>After that, though, I got lucky. The bus was brand new: it smelled of new seats, the interior was spotless, and on a huge 50-seat bus there were only five of us. The whole ride was perfectly comfortable: the air conditioning worked and, best of all, there was no Serbian music playing.</p>
<p><b>3. Podgorica</b></p>
<p>It was about 3:45 when I woke up to the harsh glare of streetlights. I looked out the window, looked at the clock. Thought: &quot;Huh, this city looks a lot like Podgorica.&quot; I&apos;d lived there for almost a year. But it&apos;s only 3:45, and we&apos;re supposed to arrive at six. Can&apos;t be.</p>
